{
  "$comment": "Shared test vectors for the engine_start contract. Checked by vitest (src/lib/params/serve.test.ts) and by cargo test (Rust engine supervisor). Hand-written: every expected argv/env/command string was written by a person, not generated by either implementation. Edit only together with both implementations.",
  "format": 1,
  "catalog": "docs/splash-params.json",
  "redacted": "••••",
  "rules": {
    "request_shape": "ServeRequest = { model: string, port: number, flags: Array<{ key: string, value: string | number | boolean | string[] | null }>, binary?: string }. `key` is a serve[].key from docs/splash-params.json.",
    "model_and_port": "model and port are top-level fields only. A flag whose key is 'model' or 'port' is an error (code 'duplicate').",
    "argv": "argv excludes the program and is: 'serve', '--model=<model>', '--port=<port>', then one or more tokens per argv flag in the order of the flags array. Never reorder flags. --port is always emitted; its min_version (1.0.1) is checked like any other flag, so Splash 1.0 cannot be launched (code 'version', key 'port').",
    "allowed_keys": "Only serve[] entries whose applies_to contains 'serve' may appear in flags (unknown or non-serve key: code 'unknown_key'). api_key and hf_token are secrets and never appear in flags (code 'secret_in_flags').",
    "version": "For each flag, min_version is compared with the detected Splash version (dotted numeric compare, missing parts are 0: '1.0' == '1.0.0'). Too new: code 'version', and the message names the flag and the version needed (e.g. '1.2.0').",
    "value_rendering": "null, false and [] are omitted. true: argv entry -> bare '--flag'; env entry -> NAME=<validation.env_value_when_on, or '1'>. string: '--flag=<value>' / NAME=<value>, rendered verbatim (TS has already canonicalised sizes like '32G' and context like '256K'). number: shortest round-trip decimal (JS String(n), Rust serde_json::Number Display): 64, 0.25, 3600. string[]: one '--flag=<item>' per item, in order.",
    "pass_as": "serve[].pass_as decides argv vs env. pass_as 'env' entries become environment variables named serve[].env and add nothing to argv.",
    "env_order": "env = flag-derived variables in flags-array order, then the secrets Rust has stored, always in the order SPLASH_API_KEY, HF_TOKEN. Secret values are reported as the redacted placeholder. Process-internal variables the supervisor adds (e.g. PYTHONUNBUFFERED) are not part of this list.",
    "command": "command = env assignments 'NAME=<value>' joined by single spaces, then 'splash', then argv, all joined by single spaces. The program is always shown as 'splash', whatever binary is used.",
    "shell_quoting": "For '--name=value' and 'NAME=value' tokens only the value part is quoted; bare tokens ('serve', '--flag') are never quoted. A value matching ^[A-Za-z0-9_@%+=:,./-]+$ is left bare; otherwise it is wrapped in single quotes with each ' written as '\\'' . An empty value is ''. The redacted placeholder is never quoted.",
    "ts_side_only": "These happen in TypeScript before the request reaches Rust, so Rust never sees them: values equal to their default are omitted; sizes/context are canonicalised (numbers are in the field's unit: GiB for max_memory/max_cache_disk, MiB for max_request_size, tokens for max_context; output is the largest of G/M/K that divides the byte count, context is NK when divisible by 1024); a flag whose validation.requires is unmet, or whose validation.disabled_when matches, is dropped; https_proxy/no_proxy always carry 127.0.0.1 and localhost in no_proxy; secrets and Splashboard-internal entries are removed.",
    "vector_fields": "version: detected Splash version. values: form values (TS buildServeRequest input; absent = Rust/render-only vector). secrets: env names of secrets the Rust side has stored. request: the ServeRequest (TS output, Rust input). argv/env/command: expected render output. env is an ordered list of [name, value] pairs (secret values already redacted); env_keys is the same list's names. error: { key, code, min_version? } - when present, TS buildServeRequest must throw it for `values`, and both TS renderServe and Rust must reject `request` with it (a vector without `request` is TS-only validation). Rust satisfies an error vector by rejecting with a message that names the key (or its --flag / env name) and, for version errors, the min_version string; `code` is the TS classification and need not appear in the Rust error."
  },
